 ],
 "lineas": [
  {
   "texto": "Look at any dam from the side, and it's shaped like a triangle. Thin on top, fat at the bottom.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "The Hoover Dam is about fourteen meters thick at the top, and two hundred at the bottom.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "That's not to save concrete. It's because water pushes harder the deeper you go.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Imagine stacking buckets of water. The bottom one carries the weight of all the water above it.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Every ten meters down, the push grows by about ten tons on each square meter.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "At the surface, the water barely leans on the dam.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "At the bottom of a deep lake, it's crushing, every second of every day.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So the base has to be thick, heavy, and wide, to keep the dam from sliding or tipping over.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "And that deep water also tries to sneak underneath.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "That's why dams have a waterproof core, and drains at the toe to let leaks out safely.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Here's the surprise. The size of the lake doesn't matter. Only the depth does.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "A narrow pond fifty meters deep pushes on its dam as hard as an ocean fifty meters deep would.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So engineers measure one number before anything else. How deep will the water get?",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "That one number decides how fat the bottom has to be.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Now you know. What should we explain next?",
   "pausa": 0.3
  }
 ],
